      g({slug:'old-city-ouchy',name:'Lausanne Old City & Ouchy',instrument:'vertical metro section',layout:'vertical-section',imageQuery:'Lausanne old city Ouchy Lake Geneva Switzerland',imageAlt:'Lausanne and Lake Geneva viewed from the hillside',summary:'Use Lausanne’s metro to connect the cathedral hill, station level and Ouchy waterfront, making elevation part of the itinerary instead of repeatedly climbing the same slope.',access:'The M2 metro links Ouchy, the station area and upper Lausanne. Start high or low and move in one direction; using the metro for one major elevation change is more efficient than proving the city is walkable.',tradeoff:'The route reveals Lausanne’s geography better than a flat attraction list. It sacrifices long museum time, so choose the cathedral/old city, Ouchy and one cultural stop rather than filling every level.',fallback:'Rain strengthens the metro-led plan: keep short old-city segments, add a museum and remove the long waterfront walk. Ouchy remains useful as a quick geographic endpoint even in poor weather.',duration:'Four to six hours covers the vertical city with a meal. Add the Olympic Museum for a full day.',combine:'Pair with Lavaux on another half day. Do not add Montreux unless Lausanne is only a transfer stop.',verify:'Check TL metro status and museum opening days. Lake boats and seasonal waterfront activities need separate confirmation.',choices:[['Top-down day','Old city first, descend via station to Ouchy; easiest on the legs.'],['Bottom-up day','Ouchy first, museum, then metro uphill for old-city evening.'],['Museum-led day','Olympic Museum plus a shorter old-town block; strongest in rain.']],stages:[['Choose a vertical direction','Decide whether the cathedral or lake is the start; do not bounce between levels.'],['Read the old city','Use the cathedral and surrounding streets as one cluster, not a repeated climb from the station.'],['Use M2 deliberately','Take the metro for the largest elevation change and protect energy for the waterfront.'],['Finish at the opposite level','End in Ouchy or the old city according to dinner and hotel location, then use transit home.']],watch:[['Hill fatigue','Lausanne’s distances look short in plan view. Elevation changes the real effort.'],['Station construction','Large station works can alter pedestrian routes and platform access. Follow current signs.'],['Lake expectations','Ouchy is a waterfront district, not a beach-resort day. Treat it as one layer of Lausanne.']]}),
      g({slug:'lavaux-terraces',name:'Lavaux Vineyard Terraces',instrument:'rail-stop terrace ladder',layout:'terrace-ladder',imageQuery:'Lavaux vineyards Lake Geneva Switzerland',imageAlt:'Lavaux vineyard terraces above Lake Geneva',summary:'Explore Lavaux as a working vineyard landscape linked by regional trains, choosing one terrace walk and a confirmed return station rather than collecting villages along the entire slope.',access:'Regional trains reach several Lavaux villages from Lausanne. Pick a start and finish station before walking; signed paths can climb or descend sharply even when the map shows a short horizontal distance.',tradeoff:'Lavaux offers landscape and cultural context rather than a packed attraction list. A longer walk gives more terrace immersion but sacrifices time for cellar visits, lakeshore or Lausanne.',fallback:'In heat, heavy rain or slippery conditions, ride between villages and make one short viewpoint walk. A scenic train corridor plus one village is a valid day; unsafe terrace hiking is not a requirement.',duration:'Half a day suits one village and a short walk; a full day works for a longer signed route plus a meal or confirmed wine visit.',combine:'Pair with Lausanne before or after only when your walking route is short. Keep Montreux/Riviera for another day because the rail line can tempt overextension.',verify:'Use SBB for the exact start/end stations, MeteoSwiss for weather and official local/UNESCO guidance for path and vineyard etiquette. Book tastings directly where required.',choices:[['Short terrace walk','One train stop to another with moderate walking; safest first visit.'],['Village-and-cellar day','Less distance, more cultural context; only with confirmed opening or booking.'],['Rail-view day','Minimal walking with selected village stops; best in heat, rain or limited mobility.']],stages:[['Choose the return station first','Lock the rail exit before entering the terraces so downhill wandering does not become a long climb back.'],['Enter on a signed path','Use public routes and respect vineyard work; private rows are not shortcuts.'],['Pause at one village','Give a real break to the settlement rather than crossing five village names for a checklist.'],['Descend or climb to the planned station','Protect the train home and avoid improvising on steep lanes when tired.']],watch:[['Working vineyards','The landscape is agricultural. Stay on public paths and do not enter rows or work areas.'],['Sun exposure','Terraces can be hot with limited shade. Water and an earlier start matter.'],['Steep shortcuts','A direct line on a map may be stairs or a sharp grade. Choose the route by mobility, not distance alone.']]}),
      g({slug:'olympic-lake-cruise',name:'Olympic Museum & Lake Geneva Day',instrument:'museum-to-pier timecard',layout:'timecard',imageQuery:'Ouchy Lake Geneva Lausanne Switzerland',imageAlt:'Ouchy waterfront on Lake Geneva in Lausanne',summary:'Build a lower-Lausanne day around the Olympic Museum and Ouchy, then add a lake boat only when its timetable creates a useful route rather than a sightseeing obligation.',access:'The M2 reaches Ouchy from the station and upper city. The Olympic Museum sits on the waterfront side; boat piers are nearby but still require checking the exact departure and destination.',tradeoff:'This is a low-stress cultural day with lake views and little climbing. It sacrifices Lausanne’s medieval core, making it best as a second day or a poor-weather plan with a clear boat window.',fallback:'If boats are canceled or impractical, keep the museum and waterfront, then return by metro for cafés or another indoor stop. The day does not fail without a cruise.',duration:'Three to five hours for museum and Ouchy; add more for a meaningful lake sector. Avoid a boat that consumes hours only to return to the same pier.',combine:'Pair with old Lausanne in the evening. A same-day Lavaux walk works only if the lake segment is very short.',verify:'Check Olympic Museum hours, current lake-navigation timetable and TL connections. Lake services are seasonal and weather-sensitive.',choices:[['Museum-first','Start indoors, then use the lakefront as a release; best in uncertain weather.'],['Lake-first','Take a useful morning boat sector, then return for the museum; best when the timetable is strong.'],['Ouchy-only','Skip the boat and keep a relaxed waterfront/cultural half day.']],stages:[['Ride down to Ouchy','Use M2 to remove the steep approach and arrive with energy for the museum.'],['Give the museum a real block','Move through the collection without watching a boat clock every twenty minutes.'],['Evaluate the lake service','Board only if the route, duration and return fit the rest of the day.'],['Return uphill by metro','Finish in central Lausanne without converting the evening into a climb.']],watch:[['Boat-as-checklist','A cruise is not automatically better than the shore. Use it only when the route adds something.'],['Museum timing','Special exhibitions and closing hours can alter the ideal order.'],['Lake weather','Wind and visibility can reduce the value of an exposed cruise even when services run.']]})
